{%- comment -%}
  Render the lookbooks that contain a given product, capped at `limit`.

  Accepts:
    product - {Object} the product to match against
    limit   - {Number} maximum lookbooks to render (default 2)

  This snippet both selects and renders. Liquid's `render` runs in an isolated scope
  and cannot return a value, so a snippet that only *computed* the matches would leave
  its caller unable to see them -- and duplicating the selection logic into the
  section to work around that is exactly how two copies drift apart. Ownership of the
  rule therefore sits here, in one place, alongside the rendering it feeds.

  The rule itself. The brief requires at most two lookbooks when a product appears in
  three or more, but does not say *which* two, and "whichever two Shopify happens to
  return" is not a rule -- admin ordering is not a contract, so it can differ between
  staging and production and shift when a merchant edits an unrelated entry.
  Selection is therefore explicit:

    1. keep lookbooks whose product_handles contains this product's handle
    2. sort ascending by the priority field
    3. tie-break ascending by metaobject handle, so equal priorities stay stable
    4. take the first `limit`

  Steps 2 and 3 happen in a single string sort. Liquid has no sort-by-key for drops,
  so each match is encoded as "PPPP:handle" -- zero-padded priority, then handle.
  Sorting those lexicographically yields numeric priority order with alphabetical
  tie-breaking, in one pass and with no nested loops.

  Zero-padding to four digits is what makes a lexicographic sort behave numerically:
  without it, "100" sorts before "20". Priorities above 9999 would break that, which
  is well beyond any plausible number of lookbooks.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign match_limit = limit | default: 2
  assign encoded_matches = ''

  for candidate in shop.metaobjects.lookbook.values
    assign candidate_handles = candidate.product_handles.value

    if candidate_handles contains product.handle
      assign priority = candidate.priority.value | default: 9999
      assign padded_priority = priority | prepend: '0000' | slice: -4, 4

      assign encoded_matches = encoded_matches | append: padded_priority | append: ':' | append: candidate.system.handle | append: ','
    endif
  endfor

  assign sorted_matches = encoded_matches | split: ',' | sort
  assign selected_matches = sorted_matches | slice: 0, match_limit
-%}

{%- if selected_matches.size > 0 -%}
  {%- for encoded in selected_matches -%}
    {%- liquid
      assign matched_handle = encoded | split: ':' | last
      assign matched_lookbook = shop.metaobjects.lookbook[matched_handle]
    -%}

    {%- if matched_lookbook != blank -%}
      {%- render 'lookbook-mount', lookbook: matched_lookbook, section: section -%}
    {%- endif -%}
  {%- endfor -%}
{%- elsif request.design_mode -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    Editor-only. On the storefront, a product belonging to no lookbook renders nothing
    at all -- that is the correct outcome, not an error worth showing a customer.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <div class="lookbook-placeholder">
    <p class="lookbook-placeholder__title">
      {{ 'sections.lookbook_product.placeholder_title' | t }}
    </p>
    <p class="lookbook-placeholder__body">
      {{ 'sections.lookbook_product.placeholder_body' | t: product: product.title }}
    </p>
  </div>
{%- endif -%}
